  "subtitle": "The first optical mouse, and the one that could not leave its printed grid pad",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum has Summagraphics Bit Pad, the GrafBar sonic pencil, and a shelf of pointing devices, but nothing whose pointer lives on a printed grid that IS the coordinates. The Mouse Systems optical mouse (Steve Kirsch, first demonstrated December 1980, sold from 1982) bounded the cursor to a special metallic pad printed with infrared-absorbing grid lines — move off the pad and it lost tracking entirely, and rotating the pad broke it. A pointing device physically chained to its own paper map, the pre-computer forebear of every LED mouse.",
  "search_terms": ["Mouse Systems optical mouse", "Steve Kirsch optical mouse 1980", "grid optical mouse pad", "first optical mouse"],
  "research": {
